// BUGS #112, second guard: a float literal is never a tuple, so `<float>.<int>`
// is always a mis-scan and must be rejected.
//
// The lexer half of the #112 fix stops `0..5` from producing a float, but it does
// not cover the shape reached any *other* way. `1.5.0` is scanned entirely
// correctly — TkFloat("1.5") TkDot TkInt(0) — and hand-writing `0. . 5` with real
// whitespace produces the same stream #112 produced by accident. Both then hit
// parse_call's numeric-dot branch, whose purpose is tuple field access, and came
// out as IndexGet(FloatLit(...), IntLit(...)): __list_get on a double, no
// diagnostic, segfault at run time.
//
// The receiver of a tuple field access can never be a float literal, so this is
// unambiguously a bug shape and parse_call rejects it by name. That is defence in
// depth, not a duplicate of the lexer guard — remove the lexer change and this
// file still fails correctly; remove this guard and `1.5.0` silently segfaults
// again.
//
// Real tuple access (`t._0`) and every genuine float form (`1.5`, `1.`, `1.e3`,
// `1.5.floor()`) are unaffected — see test/pass/scientific_notation.sf and
// test/tuple_test.sf.
//
// Expected: "cannot use '.' field access on a float literal" and exit 1.

var x = 1.5.0
IO.println(x)
